    {
      "block_id": "2605.18130_p1_b5",
      "text": "Abstract\u2014Medical image segmentation is more clinically valu-\nable when it supports diagnosis rather than merely producing\nlesion masks. However, diagnostically relevant lesion cues are of-\nten subtle and localized, while existing models may be distracted\nby background tissues, acoustic artifacts, and irrelevant visual\ncorrelations. To address this problem, we propose Rad-VLSM,\na two-stage cross-modal framework for semantics-assisted lesion\nfocusing, robust segmentation, and visually grounded diagnosis.\nIn the first stage, a BLIP-2-based vision-language alignment\nmodule identifies lesion-related candidate regions under semantic\nguidance and converts them into box prompts. In the second\nstage, these prompts are fed into a SAM-based multitask network,\nwhere a multi-candidate region aggregation strategy improves\nprompt stability and guides lesion segmentation. The predicted\nmasks are then used as spatial priors for diagnosis, and a visual-\nradiomics fusion head integrates lesion-aware visual features with\nselected radiomics descriptors. By using semantic information for\nlocalization rather than direct prediction, Rad-VLSM reduces\ntext-to-diagnosis dependence and grounds diagnosis in lesion-\nlevel evidence. Experiments on a private clinical breast ultra-\nsound dataset and public benchmarks show that Rad-VLSM\nachieves strong segmentation and diagnostic performance with\nfavorable generalization.\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.96,
        177.11,
        300.02,
        405.25
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        195,
        708,
        1200,
        1621
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 8.97,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-MediItal",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 1411,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
