    {
      "block_id": "2605.18130_p1_b7",
      "text": "I. INTRODUCTION\n",
      "bbox": [
        135.62,
        448.41,
        213.38,
        458.38
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        542,
        1793,
        853,
        1833
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 15,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.18130_p1_b8",
      "text": "Medical imaging plays a pivotal role in disease screening,\nsubtype diagnosis, and treatment planning, while accurate\nlesion segmentation remains one of its most fundamental\ntechnical components [1], [2], [3].\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.96,
        463.46,
        300.02,
        509.29
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        195,
        1853,
        1200,
        2037
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 208,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.18130_p1_b9",
      "text": "Recent deep learning models have achieved high-quality\nmasks across a variety of medical segmentation tasks, and the\nemergence of foundation models has further improved their\nadaptability across imaging scenarios [4], [5], [6]. However, in\nreal clinical workflows, a high-quality mask is not the ultimate\ngoal. What clinicians truly need is a model that can not only\ndelineate the lesion precisely, but also make a reliable diag-\nnostic judgment and explicitly indicate the evidence supporting\nthat judgment, thereby forming a diagnostic loop grounded in\nspatial evidence [7], [8], [9]. Therefore, beyond developing\nisolated segmentation or classification models, it is clinically\nmeaningful to build a unified framework that connects lesion\nlocalization, segmentation, and diagnosis [10], [11], [12].\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.96,
        511.23,
        300.02,
        664.65
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        195,
        2044,
        1200,
        2658
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 801,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.18130_p1_b10",
      "text": "This\nchallenge\nbecomes\neven\nmore\npronounced\nin\nultrasound-based lesion analysis. In breast ultrasound images,\nlesions often occupy only a small fraction of the image\nand are frequently accompanied by weak boundaries, low\ncontrast, heterogeneous internal echoes, speckle noise, and\nacoustic shadows, making reliable lesion-level representation\ndifficult [13], [14], [15]. Meanwhile, surrounding glandular\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.96,
        666.6,
        300.02,
        748.29
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        195,
        2666,
        1200,
        2993
